WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:00.958
(audience applause)
- No matter how much

00:00:00.958 --> 00:00:03.600
you dream and work to
succeed in show business,

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.120
you've also gotta figure
out how to pay those bills.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.210
We heard Ben Vereen say just
that a little while ago.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.370
Trust me, I was a cocktail
waitress, I had so many jobs.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.230
Served food, sold popcorn
at a movie theater,

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.760
sang and danced at Six Flags Over Texas.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.710
Like literally, I've worked so many jobs.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:19.470
But those jobs were low-key
compared to some of the work

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.630
that others have done on
their rise to the top.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.070
So check this out.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.110
- Gosh, I taught preschool for five years

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.224
and that was so much fun.

00:00:27.224 --> 00:00:28.260
(audience applauds)

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.220
- You were a telemarketer?

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.150
- Oh, you were a telemarketer, too?

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.620
- I was.
- Oh, dang!

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.290
- I worked in a shoe store,
and a fast food joint,

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.410
and a, you know, an auto body
shop, and sold newspaper.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.230
- I used to work at the Four
Seasons Hotel in Beverly Hills.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.790
Yeah, I worked there
seven and a half years,

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.470
waited on all of Hollywood.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.210
- I lifeguarded for years, actually,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.080
from the age of 16 well
into my mid-twenties.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.490
- Is it true that back in the eighties

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.100
that you were a breakdancer?

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:53.933
- Let tell you something.

00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:55.193
- [Kelly] Let me tell you something!

00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:58.530
- Matt Damon suggested that he and I-

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.300
- He threw him under that
bus so fast! (laughs)

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.680
- Oh, you know, hardly.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.070
We had some nylon Puma suits.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:04.903
- [Kelly] Oh my God. (chuckles)

00:01:04.903 --> 00:01:08.730
- We wanted to be cool, and so
we learned how to breakdance.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.107
And then Matt was like, "If
we just get some linoleum

00:01:11.107 --> 00:01:12.787
"and put it down at the subway,

00:01:12.787 --> 00:01:14.557
"we could do some of our moves, you know,

00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:16.020
"and like put out a hat."

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.290
So it is not a lie to say that he and I,

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.390
and a few other people who I won't name

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.500
because they have families

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.555
and they're somewhere in Boston now.

00:01:24.555 --> 00:01:25.410
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.490
- You made money breakdancing.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.697
- Well, I won't say anybody
put quarters in the cup.

00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:30.530
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.010
I think they walked by with
pity and astonishment, like.

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:35.350
- Um, (exhales strongly),

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.100
it's kind of traumatic when I talk about,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.710
but like, but in a good way.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.980
So basically I'm a fourth
generation dog shower.

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:44.910
- Yeah, that's a big deal!

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.870
- My great-grandmother started
the Beagle Club of Australia.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.730
I am allergic to dogs though,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.950
which is where the tragedy comes in.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.950
So every weekend, every weekend,
we would go to dog shows

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:00.180
and I'd be like, (strangled
noise) like that,

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.820
like that's what my fun childhood.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:03.995
And my family-

00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:05.180
- [Kelly] What kind of irony is that?

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.050
- So my family would,

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:09.030
how we'd make money is we had a little,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.920
there's me with all
the dog showing ribbons

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.960
that I won behind me.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.210
And yeah, there's me at
some of the dog shows.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.200
Look how young I am,

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.370
I'm like literally three
years old showing the beagles.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.970
And my mom was devastated
when I didn't continue

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.670
the family legacy of being a dog shower.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.927
She's literally, I had to
tell her in a public place,

00:02:28.927 --> 00:02:30.934
"I think I'm gonna be an actress",

00:02:30.934 --> 00:02:32.017
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:35.970
because she wanted me to be a dog shower.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.280
She was devastated!

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.350
And for my first few TV shows,

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:41.910
she wouldn't even put them on in the house

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.170
cause she was so upset. (laughs)

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.130
- Oh my God!

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.390
- Now she's fine with it,

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.370
she's fine with my career choices now.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.592
But also if you're watching-

00:02:48.592 --> 00:02:49.425
- And now you're doing "Pooch Perfect".

00:02:49.425 --> 00:02:51.000
- Do what you want,
don't listen to your mom.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.640
- Don't listen! (laughs)

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.380
- I ran the business center
at a hotel a long time ago.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.130
No, the hotel no longer
exists, probably because of me.

00:03:02.202 --> 00:03:03.270
(Nick laughs)

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.920
But the job was brutal

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.250
because I had to be
there in a suit and tie,

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.750
ready to see anyone.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.310
Unlock, open up the office,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:15.750
warm up the copy machine, the
fax, everything at 6:00 AM.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.890
And I was coming from like
35 minutes away on the train.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.320
So, you know, it was an early start

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.320
and no one would ever show
up until at least 8:00 AM.

00:03:27.895 --> 00:03:28.786
(Kelly groans)

00:03:28.786 --> 00:03:31.530
So I actually, I developed
a great kind of system

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.370
of getting there, opening everything up,

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.170
turning on the lights, warming up the fax,

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.190
getting everything going.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:39.960
And then I would take off my jacket,

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.480
loosen my tie, unbutton the top button,

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.940
and take out a small pillow
from one of my drawers,

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.130
put it on my desk, lean over,

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.110
and I'd be out for two hours.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:53.450
I just learned to how to
sleep like this, basically-

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.996
(Kelly and Nick laugh)

00:03:55.996 --> 00:03:56.829
- That's awesome!

00:03:56.829 --> 00:03:59.610
- And then I developed
like crazy good hearing.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.550
As soon as I would hear
the door start to open

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.040
I was like, up! Shnk! Boom! Coat! Bam!

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.097
And pretend like-
- You're like, "What!

00:04:06.097 --> 00:04:08.733
"No one was sleeping, I've been awake!"

